<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narnala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narnala is a hill fortress in the Satpura range in the Akola district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Of early origin, it was rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and strengthened by the Imad Shahi sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, who held it alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gawilgarh">Gawilgarh</a>. Its long walls, gates and Persian inscriptions survive, and it later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narnala.jpg" alt="Narnala" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Gill · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narnala_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
